      <h1>Uber Accident Lawyer in San Francisco: Who Pays</h1>
      <div class="entry-content">
<p>Who pays after an Uber or Lyft crash in San Francisco depends on where the driver was in the app when the collision happened. During a trip, the company's commercial policy generally responds. When the app is open but no ride has been accepted, the company still has to provide primary coverage, at lower limits. When the app is off, the driver's own policy governs, and many personal policies exclude for-hire driving. That one detail decides which adjuster takes your call.</p>
<p>Plenty of people start looking for an Uber accident lawyer in San Francisco after a claims representative tells them the company is not responsible. Sometimes that is true. Often it is just early.</p>
<h2 id="the-three-coverage-periods-that-decide-who-pays">The three coverage periods that decide who pays</h2>
<p>Two or three policies can sit over the same crash. California treats Uber and Lyft as Transportation Network Companies. The <a href="https://www.cpuc.ca.gov/tncinfo">CPUC</a> licenses them and sets their insurance requirements. Its 2013 decision required at least $1 million per incident for crashes involving a TNC vehicle in transit to or during a trip. The three-period framework that surrounds that number was set in 2015, and the periods are worth learning by name because they decide your claim.</p>
<p>The $1 million figure gets repeated everywhere. The part that decides your claim is when it attaches, and California answers that with three defined periods.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>App status at the moment of impact</th><th>What California requires</th><th>Who to claim against</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>App off, not logged in</td><td>No TNC coverage at all. Only the driver's personal auto policy, and many personal policies exclude for-hire use.</td><td>The driver personally, plus your own uninsured or underinsured motorist coverage</td></tr><tr><td>Period 1: app open, waiting for a match</td><td>Primary coverage of $50,000 per person, $100,000 per incident and $30,000 for property damage, plus $200,000 excess. The company may meet this using the driver's own policy if it carries TNC coverage. A standard personal policy that excludes for-hire use does not count.</td><td>The company's insurer first, and the driver's personal insurer as a second target</td></tr><tr><td>Period 2: request accepted, driving to pickup</td><td>$1,000,000 primary commercial coverage</td><td>The company's insurer, which also covers the driver for that trip</td></tr><tr><td>Period 3: passenger in the car until drop-off</td><td>$1,000,000 primary liability, plus uninsured and underinsured motorist coverage of $60,000 per person and $300,000 per incident for the passenger</td><td>The company's insurer</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>That $60,000/$300,000 figure is current. <a href="https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?sectionNum=5433.&amp;lawCode=PUC">Senate Bill 371</a>, effective January 1, 2026, reduced the passenger-period uninsured and underinsured motorist requirement from $1,000,000 to those amounts. The $1,000,000 primary liability requirement did not change. Plenty of pages online, including the <a href="https://www.cpuc.ca.gov/tncinsurance">CPUC's own insurance summary</a>, still show the older million-dollar UM/UIM number, so check the date on anything you read.</p>
<p>The middle rows are where these cases are won and lost, and the rule surprises people. California does not leave the gap to the driver's personal policy. The company has to provide primary coverage from the moment the driver logs in. The one exception is a driver who bought a rideshare endorsement on their own policy, which the company is allowed to rely on instead. Most drivers have not. So when the app was on and nobody had been matched yet, the company's coverage usually leads, and the personal insurer's denial is a side issue rather than the main event.</p>
<p>Now the part most posts skip. Coverage is not liability. A $1 million policy behind the car does not mean the company owes you $1 million, or that it owes you anything. The $1 million is also per incident, not per person. If four people were hurt in that car, they are arguing over the same pot. You still have to prove the driver was negligent and that the negligence caused your injuries. What the policy does is give the claim somewhere to land. In a crash with an uninsured driver in a borrowed car, there is sometimes nowhere to send the claim but your own policy.</p>
<h2 id="if-you-were-a-passenger">If you were a passenger</h2>
<p>Passenger claims are usually the cleanest version of this case. Not because the company is generous, but because two things are rarely in dispute: the ride was active, and you did not cause the crash.</p>
<p>The claim goes to the company's insurer for the period when the ride was matched, the $1 million layer under the CPUC rules. It covers the driver's liability for your injuries: medical costs, lost income, pain and suffering, subject to proof. It is not a no-fault benefit, so you still build a file.</p>
<p>Your own auto policy may help even if you were not in your car. Medical payments and uninsured motorist coverage can follow you as a passenger, depending on the policy language. Keep your health insurer informed too.</p>
<p>Expect the company to ask for a recorded statement and a medical authorization within days. Neither is required for the claim to move forward. A statement given before you know how bad the injury is can be hard to walk back.</p>
<h2 id="if-the-other-driver-caused-the-crash">If the other driver caused the crash</h2>
<p>Then the rideshare angle mostly disappears. Your claim runs against the driver who hit the car you were riding in. California requires drivers to carry at least $30,000 per person and $60,000 per accident in bodily injury liability, plus $15,000 for property damage. The <a href="https://www.insurance.ca.gov/01-consumers/105-type/95-guides/01-auto/">California Department of Insurance</a> publishes consumer guides on how those limits and your own coverage interact.</p>
<p>$30,000 does not go far after an ER visit and imaging. So what else exists:</p>
<ul><li>Underinsured motorist coverage on a policy in your household, which can pay the gap when the at-fault driver's limits are too low.</li><li>A commercial policy, if the at-fault driver was working at the time.</li><li>A second defendant, such as an employer whose worker was driving, or an owner who lent the car.</li></ul>
<p>The TNC policy for your own driver is not the answer here. It covers your driver's liability, not the other driver's fault. If the other car caused the crash, your San Francisco <a href="/car-accidents-lawyer-san-francisco/">car accident claim</a> follows the other car.</p>
<h2 id="if-you-were-driving-for-uber-or-lyft">If you were driving for Uber or Lyft</h2>
<p>This is the gap nobody warns drivers about. Coverage during a matched trip is one thing. Coverage for the other twenty hours of the day is your own responsibility, and it may not exist.</p>
<p>Personal auto policies commonly exclude commercial use, livery, or carrying passengers for a fee. The wording varies by carrier, so read the exclusions page rather than trusting a phone answer. If the app was off when the crash happened, the personal insurer may deny the claim outright, and you may end up defending a lawsuit with your own assets.</p>
<p>Three things worth doing before the next shift:</p>
<ol><li>Read the exclusions section and ask your agent, in writing, whether the policy covers rideshare driving.</li><li>Ask about a rideshare endorsement. Some carriers offer one that adds coverage for app-on periods.</li><li>Save the trip receipt and app screenshots for any shift where something happens. The clock on data starts immediately.</li></ol>
<p>Your own injuries are a separate issue. Whether the commercial policy pays you as a driver depends on its terms, including uninsured motorist coverage. Ask rather than assume.</p>
<h2 id="what-an-uber-accident-lawyer-in-san-francisco-asks-for-first">What an Uber accident lawyer in San Francisco asks for first</h2>
<p>Most of what decides these claims is not at the scene. It sits in the company's database, and only the company has it.</p>
<p>The trip record is the anchor. It shows when the request came in, when the driver arrived, when the trip began and ended, and whether it was cancelled. Add the GPS trace, the driver's status log, and in-app messages about the ride. Those timestamps answer the coverage question better than anyone's memory.</p>
<p>Then the ordinary evidence: dashcam footage from either car, nearby business cameras, the police report, witness names, and the damage pattern on both vehicles. If you were in the back seat, a photo of the trade dress on the windshield helps confirm the car was working for the app at the time.</p>
<p>The company will not hand this over on request. A written preservation letter, sent early and specific about the records you want, keeps the data from being overwritten. Assume it has a short life. For the broader checklist, see <a href="/blog/evidence-after-a-san-francisco-car-accident/">what evidence matters after a San Francisco car accident</a> and this guide to <a href="/blog/digital-evidence-in-personal-injury-cases-what-you-need-to-know/">digital evidence in injury cases</a>.</p>
<h2 id="what-to-do-in-the-first-48-hours">What to do in the first 48 hours</h2>
<ol><li>Report the crash. Call 911 if anyone is hurt, and get the report number.</li><li>Photograph both vehicles, the plates, the driver, the trade dress, and any visible injury.</li><li>Screenshot your app: the receipt, the driver's name, the trip ID, the time. Do it before the app updates the record.</li><li>Get examined the same day or the next. Neck and back injuries often show up late.</li><li>Report it through the app as well, and save the confirmation email.</li><li>Write down what you remember, including anything the driver said about the app or the trip.</li><li>Be careful with recorded statements. You can decline and ask for the request in writing.</li><li>Stay off social media about the crash.</li></ol>
<p>One deadline sits over all of it. Under <a href="https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=CCP&amp;sectionNum=335.1">Code of Civil Procedure § 335.1</a>, you have two years from the date of injury to file a lawsuit in California. That sounds generous. It is not, because witnesses and camera footage age faster than the deadline.</p>
<h2 id="why-the-first-offer-comes-early-and-low">Why the first offer comes early and low</h2>
<p>An adjuster will usually call before treatment is finished. That is not an accident. Nobody knows yet whether the disc injury needs surgery or whether the trip log will contradict the company's first position.</p>
<p>The offer that arrives in week two is priced on the assumption that you will heal. It often lands near the bills you have already run up, in exchange for a full release. Sign it and the claim is generally closed, even if the MRI in month three shows something serious.</p>
<p>Two more things happen early. The company says its driver was not in a covered period, and the personal insurer raises a for-hire exclusion. Both can be honest positions and still be wrong on the record. Until someone pulls the trip log, nobody has proved anything.</p>
<p>So the sequence matters. Confirm the coverage period. Preserve the data. Finish treatment. Then talk numbers.</p>
<h2 id="the-short-version">The short version</h2>
<p>Rideshare claims are document fights wearing the costume of car accident claims. The crash is usually straightforward. The fight is over which policy responds and what the app recorded in the ninety seconds before impact.</p>
<p>Our San Francisco office is at 369 Pine Street in the Financial District. If you want a straight read on where your claim stands, call (415) 360-5266. You can also read about <a href="/personal-injury-lawyer-san-francisco/">personal injury representation</a> first.</p>
<p>This is general information, not legal advice about your case. Coverage and deadlines depend on facts we have not reviewed.</p>
<h2 id="frequently-asked-questions">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<h3 id="who-is-liable-after-an-uber-or-lyft-accident-in-san-francisco">Who is liable after an Uber or Lyft accident in San Francisco?</h3>
<p>Liability depends on who was negligent, not on who owns the app. If the rideshare driver caused the crash during a matched trip, the claim is generally presented to the company's commercial insurer, which has to meet the CPUC's $1 million per-incident minimum. If another driver caused it, that driver is the defendant. Fault and coverage are separate questions.</p>
<h3 id="does-the-company-s-insurance-cover-me-if-the-driver-s-app-was-off">Does the company's insurance cover me if the driver's app was off?</h3>
<p>Usually not through the commercial policy. With the app off, the driver is in a personal vehicle, so the personal auto policy is generally the only coverage on the driver's side. Many personal policies exclude for-hire use, so the insurer may deny coverage entirely. Your own uninsured motorist coverage may then become the practical route.</p>
<h3 id="can-i-sue-uber-or-lyft-directly">Can I sue Uber or Lyft directly?</h3>
<p>You can bring a claim against the company, but the realistic path is usually through its insurer under the policy it is required to carry. California law and the company's contracts shape what can be argued about the driver's status. Whether the company itself bears responsibility depends on the facts, and it is not automatic.</p>
<h3 id="what-if-the-uber-driver-was-not-at-fault">What if the Uber driver was not at fault?</h3>
<p>Then your claim runs against the at-fault driver and that driver's insurance. The rideshare company's policy covers its driver's liability, not the other motorist's. Check for underinsured motorist coverage on any auto policy in your household, since California's minimum limits are often too low for a serious injury.</p>
<h3 id="how-long-do-i-have-to-file-a-rideshare-injury-claim-in-california">How long do I have to file a rideshare injury claim in California?</h3>
<p>Two years from the date of injury under Code of Civil Procedure § 335.1. Claims involving a public entity, such as a city vehicle or a dangerous intersection, run on a much shorter administrative deadline. Negotiation usually starts well before the filing date, so waiting is rarely harmless.</p>
<h3 id="do-i-need-my-own-car-insurance-to-file-a-passenger-claim">Do I need my own car insurance to file a passenger claim?</h3>
<p>No. The claim against the rideshare company's policy does not depend on you owning a car. But if you or a household member carries auto insurance, medical payments or uninsured motorist coverage may add another source of recovery. Ask for the declarations page and read what applies to passengers.</p>
